# Gitleaks fingerprint ignore file.
#
# Each line is a gitleaks fingerprint string (format:
# `<commit-sha>:<file-path>:<rule-id>:<line-number>`) that suppresses
# a known false-positive — typically a realistic-but-fake key in a
# test fixture or example.
#
# How fingerprints work (gitleaks v8.x):
#
# - The fingerprint is **commit-scoped**: it incorporates the commit
#   SHA where the match was found, plus the file path, rule ID, and
#   line number. Each new commit that contains a matching string gets
#   its own distinct fingerprint, so editing a file does NOT
#   automatically invalidate prior entries.
# - This means a `.gitleaksignore` entry **permanently** suppresses
#   one specific (commit, file, rule, line) combination. If the
#   fixture moves to a new line (or a new commit), gitleaks emits a
#   new finding that requires a separate review and a new entry.
# - The CI workflow runs `actions/checkout` with `fetch-depth: 0`,
#   so the full history is scanned. An entry suppressing an old
#   commit's finding stays in effect across re-scans.
#
# To add an entry after a known false positive:
#   1. Run `gitleaks detect --source . --verbose` locally.
#   2. Copy the offending fingerprint from the output (already in the
#      correct format).
#   3. Add the line here with a comment explaining why the match is
#      a false positive.
#   4. Get reviewer sign-off — gitleaks-bypass entries are
#      reviewer-visible by design.
#
# **On positive findings in production code**: under the free private
# plan there is no push protection, so a real key has reached
# GitHub.com infrastructure even if the PR is closed unmerged.
# Rotate the key immediately, then add a `.gitleaksignore` entry only
# if a legitimate fixture is the source. Never add an ignore entry
# without rotating the key first when there's any chance the value
# is real.
